    "narrative": "The offensive core for Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily on speed-indexed upside today, anchored by **James Wood** (77.49) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91). **James Wood** provides the highest floor, benefiting from an elite .422 xwOBA and a favorable matchup against Ritchie, while **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits from a 10% park boost and a high-leverage spot in the #2 hole against Montero. **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) rounds out the top trio; despite a slight penalty for facing an elite holder of stolen bases, his 14% boost from dynamic platoon conditions and significant park factors make him a high-reward UTIL start. **Brice Turang** (70.78) remains an essential play, as his leadoff status and superior SP-adjusted outlook against Ure\u00f1a make him a primary engine for our SB ambitions.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved choosing between **Mickey Moniak** and **Zach McKinstry** (49.14). While **Zach McKinstry** possesses an appealing 10% park boost and stolen base upside, the optimizer favored **Mickey Moniak**'s significantly stronger platoon advantage and superior SP-matched efficiency. Elsewhere, **Corbin Carroll** (59.15) faces a stiff test against Williams, G; while his speed is a critical component of our 5x5 strategy, he faces a sub-3.00 xERA pitcher and a restrictive catcher pop-time, warranting caution. We are also monitoring **Jose Caballero** (52.02), whose score is tethered to a negative wind effect and a difficult platoon matchup. We continue to hold **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Austin Riley**, **Cole Young**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Max Muncy**, and **Edwin Arroyo** in reserve; **Austin Riley** was the closest to cracking the lineup, but his suboptimal order position and the current pitching-heavy environment pushed him to the bench in favor of more efficient platoon-advantaged bats.",
